<div class="row">
    <div class="col-xs-12">
        <div class="page-heading-logo">
            <div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="page-heading text-right">
            <h1 class="main-heading">
                Test Bed [Samples]
            </h1>
            <h4 class="sub-heading">
                <span class="margin-bottom-15">
                    Modular Slicers: building as KO components, N interactive capability
                </span>
            </h4>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="row">
    <div class="col-xs-12">
        <p>
            Great, we've got the extensiblility of this slicer concept defined nicely. We can have a collection of slicers put
            together in a matter of minutes, and rendered in seven lines of markup or fewer. Their interactions are all coherent.
            We can define a computed which will inform us of any changes, when changes have stopped.
        </p>
        <p>
            The next problem is that of data. At the moment, to arrive at those colours which a particular brand's products are
            available in involves touching dbo.Product, and this approach will potentially lead to <i>many</i> touches to dbo.Product in succession. In order to alleviate this we wish there to be, specific to given groups of slicers' requirements, large cartesian product tables holding all of the combinations of data which we might want to query.
        </p>
        <p>
            That can quickly become confusing. I'm slightly confused already.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

